{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The pointwise hypothesis is reversed. The source requires S ⊢ K(Kφ → F(⌜φ⌝)): F captures everything absolutely provable. Lean requires 𝓢 ⊢ K(F φ → K φ): everything F captures is absolutely provable."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Lean omits the requirement that S includes EA. Even at the level of theorem inclusion, it does not require 𝓑 ⊢ φ to imply 𝓢 ⊢ φ. Def1 gives only 𝓢 ⊢ K φ, and Def4 gives known factivity, not an unboxed factivity principle that would let 𝓢 infer φ."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Lean adds the substantive premise 𝓑 ⊢ F(∼F ⊥) → F ⊥. This is the author's acknowledged proviso, but it restricts the result to operators satisfying a base-provable incompleteness implication that the printed theorem does not assume."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The declared abstraction replaces EA and its language with an arbitrary classical propositional base and sentence type. An arbitrary operation F on sentences need not come from a one-free-variable arithmetic formula applied to sentence codes; arithmetic syntax, quotation and object-language quantifiers are not represented."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lean explicitly assumes base knowledge, closure of K under modus ponens, and known factivity. The displayed source theorem does not separately enumerate these background principles for absolute provability."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lean fixes Con(F) to mean ∼F ⊥, an interpretation not specified in the quoted passage. Under that reading, the nesting of K and negation in the conclusion is correct."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The conclusion matches under the stated reading of Con(F), but the central hypothesis says that K captures F rather than that F captures K. The missing theory inclusion and the added incompleteness premise also change the theorem's conditions."
}
